SOLUTION
Here is the step-by-step guide to setting up the parameterized Jenkins job for your new DevOps Engineer. It is a good practice to take screenshots at each major step for documentation.

🛠️ Step 1: Create the Parameterized Job
Log in to the Jenkins UI using the credentials admin / Adm!n321.

From the main dashboard, click New Item.

Enter the name parameterized-job and select Freestyle project, then click OK.

⚙️ Step 2: Add the String Parameter (Stage)
On the job configuration page, scroll to the General section.

Check the box for This project is parameterized.

Click Add Parameter and select String Parameter.

Configure it as follows:

Name: Stage

Default Value: Build

(Optional) Description: Specify the stage name.

📋 Step 3: Add the Choice Parameter (env)
Click Add Parameter again and select Choice Parameter.

Configure it as follows:

Name: env

Choices: Enter the following values, one per line:

Development

Staging

Production

(Optional) Description: Select the environment.

💻 Step 4: Configure the Shell Command
Scroll down to the Build section and click Add build step.

Select Execute shell.

In the Command text area, enter a script to echo both parameter values. For example:

bash
echo "Stage: $Stage"
echo "Environment: $env"
This uses the parameter names as variables ($Stage and $env) to access the values passed during the build.

✅ Step 5: Save and Build with the "Production" Parameter
Click Save at the bottom of the configuration page.

On the job's dashboard, click Build with Parameters (do not use "Build Now").

On the build form:

Leave the Stage parameter as its default (Build).

From the env dropdown, select Production.

Click Build.

🔍 Step 6: Verify the Build Success
In the Build History on the left, click the latest build number (e.g., #1).

Click Console Output.

You should see output similar to the following, confirming the parameters were passed correctly and the job completed successfully:

text
Stage: Build
Environment: Production
Finished: SUCCESS

💡 A Note on Plugins & Restarts
The steps above use core Jenkins features and should not require additional plugins. However, if you are prompted to install plugins or restart Jenkins during the process, you can safely click "Restart Jenkins when installation is complete and no jobs are running" on the Update Center page. If the UI appears stuck during the restart, simply refresh your browser page to regain access.

